Original commit body from c3b73c036a07223fab4163f6500cdc5f798557fc:

Corrects four places that carry the same wrong claim, all merged earlier today:
the 0006 correction section, the measurements index, the CHANGELOG entry, and
the comment block in erlang.yaml.

WHAT I CLAIMED. That measurement 0006's three adjudicated rabbitmq true
positives "re-root onto io:format" when the io_lib row is removed, so ADR-0049
ruling 3's represented-crossing test was met, S1 was void as a deduction, and
useful precision was unchanged at 24.1%.

WHAT THE PANEL FOUND. Two independent blind passes per repository, given the
flow and the source but not the prior labels, walled off from every ledger and
prior measurement. DISAGREEMENT 0 OF 7. All four rabbitmq situations are FALSE
POSITIVES, and both passes derived the same structural refutation without
conferring: the re-rooted route terminates at rabbit_misc:module_attributes/1,
whose io:format (rabbit_misc.erl:731) has exactly one argument list --
[Module] -- bound at :755-762 from application:get_key(App, modules). Neither
parameter of module_attributes_from_apps/2 reaches it. Each panellist then
searched independently for a real chain and found none; the real formatting
runs through rabbit_misc:format/2 = lists:flatten(io_lib:format(...)) and is
handed to ?LOG_* macros, which are logger:.

So the removal replaced a VACUOUS TRUE POSITIVE with an OUTRIGHT FALSE ONE.
S1's deduction STANDS and useful precision under it is 21.4% (24/112) -- the
figure 0006 published before I tried to correct it.

NOT RETRACTED: io_lib:format returns an iolist and writes nothing; the owner's
ruling against the row; the removal itself, which deleted vacuous findings; the
derived adjacency gate; and the javascript path.* finding. Only the LICENCE
ARGUMENT is withdrawn -- for these three situations there is no real crossing
to represent.

THE LESSON. The A/B was run correctly: both arms cold on separate
XDG_CACHE_HOME with the cold line asserted, a six-claim control, catalogue
restored and verified by sha. What an A/B over TOOL OUTPUT cannot do is
establish that a finding is TRUE. Presence of a finding after a change is not
evidence the finding is correct, and re-rooting is not relocation unless the
destination chain is real. An A/B prices a CHANGE; only adjudication prices a
CLAIM.

Also lands the paired precision study that produced this, run on the current
tree with the analyzer SHA recorded per ADR-0048 A1: 97 of 0006's 112
situations survive, correctness precision 33.9% -> 29.9%, useful precision
24.1% -> 23.7%. Flat on useful, down on correctness. Five of the eleven
vacuity deductions are genuinely fixed; six never left (INV-kosur).

